AB  - Rice feeds an estimated 3.5 billion people worldwide, making it central to global food security. Early detection of disease is essential to protect yields, and augmented reality (AR) offers unique potential through hands-free, firstperson data capture that can deliver real-time diagnostic support in the field. Yet AR-based disease recognition remains constrained by dataset scarcity, as infections are seasonal, environmentally dependent, and costly to annotate. To address this gap, we introduce a pipeline that uses DALL E 3 generative editing to synthesize diseased rice leaves from healthy AR captures. The resulting synthetic datasets were used to train a YOLOv11n model and benchmarked against real diseased AR images. The model achieved strong performance $(\text{mAP} {@} 0.50=0.78)$, confirming that AI-generated lesions preserved diagnostic traits relevant for robust detection. By providing a data-efficient pathway toward smarter crop health monitoring, this research supports Sustainable Development Goal 2 (Zero Hunger) and advances sustainable food security through technological innovation.
ER  - 

